Thanks @HypeMC for the quick catch and the precise diagnosis. I reworked the branch to remove the limitation instead of guarding against it, so wrapping keeps working on wide terminals rather than switching off above 120 columns.

The key fact is that PCRE only duplicates bounded repetitions of groups; a bounded repetition of a single character compiles to a counter of constant size, valid up to 65535. So `wrap()` now folds each tag and URL to a single unused private-use codepoint, wraps with `.{1,width}`, and restores them at the end. Each tag and each URL already counted as one repetition unit, so the semantics are unchanged.

What I checked:

- a differential fuzz over 23200 cases (tags, URLs, multibyte, tabs, CRLF, over-long words, both `allowCutUrls` modes) at widths 1 to 136 produces byte-for-byte the same output as the previous pattern;
- the full Console test suite passes;
- widths from 137 up to and beyond the quantifier limit now wrap instead of failing, and `preg_replace()` no longer returns `null`, which used to swallow the text being wrapped.

I flipped the new descriptor test accordingly: a 200-column terminal now asserts wrapped output, and `OutputWrapperTest` covers the two widths that used to fail. I also updated the description to match.

